## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design multi-tenant inference with dynamic schemas, deterministic preprocessing, and zero-downtime schema updates.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped schema registry with versioning
- Runtime feature alignment and type casting
- Per-tenant batching and routing to model versions
- Graceful schema hot-reload and in-flight request integrity
- gRPC path to TF Serving and error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: load tenant schema and map fields to tensor order
function preprocess(input, tenantId, version){
  const schema = registry.get(tenantId, version);
  const ordered = schema.fields.map(f => input[f] ?? schema.defaults[f]);
  // cast types, handle missing, etc.
  return tf.tensor(ordered);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor tail latency per-tenant and detect schema drift in this setup?
- What consistency model would you enforce for schema updates across replicas?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design live vocabulary updates without downtime and without compromising inference determinism or latency.

## Key Concepts
- Online feature/versioning strategy
- Atomic swaps and read/write separation
- Embedding vocab management for high-cardinality features
- Rollback, monitoring, and drift testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: atomic vocab switch in a minimal preprocessing shim
class VocabCache {
  constructor(a,b) { this.current = 'A'; this.maps = { A: a, B: b }; }
  lookup(cat){ const map = this.maps[this.current]; return map[cat] ?? map['<UNK>']; }
  swapVersion(next) { this.current = next; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift and decide when to promote vocab?
- How would you test hot-swap under burst traffic and ensure P95 latency stays within target?
